Here is the full poem:
"No man is an island, entire of itself;
Every man is a piece of the continent, a part of the main;
If a clod be washed away by the sea, Europe is the less,
As well as if a promontory were, as well as if a manor of thy friend's or of thine own were;
Any man's death diminishes me, because I am involved in mankind;
And therefore never send to know for whom the bell tolls;
It tolls for thee."
John Donne (1572-1631) was an English poet, satirist, and Anglican priest. He is considered one of the greatest poets of the English language and is known for his metaphysical poetry, which often deals with themes of death, religion, and the interconnectedness of humanity.

